158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (Drawing on the two studies discussed above, we identify two possible pathways through which advanced AI may weaken human agency. The first concerns changes in economic relationships, while the second focuses on increasing dependence on AI for knowledge and decision-making. These mechanisms help us understand where early signs of declining human influence might emerge and provide context for the questions explored in the roundtables.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 80 0 R
/T (Possible pathways towards declining human agency)
/E (Possible pathways towards declining human agency)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (The first pathway relates to a central concern in the human claims roundtable, which is how institutions might respond to human interests if they become less economically dependent on people. The second extends this concern to decision-making, where people may retain formal authority while becoming increasingly dependent on processes they cannot meaningfully inspect or challenge. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (These mechanisms may also reinforce one another. As economic leverage declines, people may have less power to demand accountability, while increasing reliance on AI for decision-making may limit their ability to understand or challenge how resources are distributed and whose interests institutions prioritise.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
