179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (The first roundtable revealed substantial disagreement about what, if anything, humans could rely on if they no longer contributed economically in ways that powerful institutions needed, and if they could not enforce their claims directly. Several participants distinguished human value as a moral or social question from human leverage as a question of power, emphasising that recognising human interests as important does not necessarily mean that those interests will be protected or respected.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (One participant drew on the contrasting experiences of resource-rich states to illustrate how governments' incentives to support their populations may depend on how wealth is generated. When economic production relies on a healthy, educated workforce, governments have practical reasons to invest in people's wellbeing. Where substantial wealth comes from natural resources, this dependence may weaken, although the benefits can still be widely shared.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
