/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Another participant raised the example of communities that have deliberately rejected or limited their use of particular technologies. These communities demonstrate that opting out is not necessarily a rejection of social participation, but a choice about the terms under which technology enters collective life.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (In a post-control scenario, the practical ability to refuse an AI-mediated arrangement becomes an important test of autonomy. If essential services, economic opportunities or social participation depend on systems people cannot avoid, formal consent offers little protection.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (Preserving meaningful alternatives therefore involves more than allowing individuals to express preferences. It requires arrangements under which refusal does not automatically exclude people from the resources or relationships necessary for participation in society.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
