/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (A further question concerned how systems designed to support human decisions could preserve agency as more complex tasks were delegated. Delegation can improve access to information and expand what people are able to do, but it can also make the criteria by which options are selected less visible and leave individuals dependent on processes they cannot inspect or reverse. The relevant distinction is between participation that genuinely affects outcomes and participation that merely gives the appearance of choice after the decisive conditions have been determined elsewhere.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 84 0 R
/E (The discussion connects with the knowledge and decision-making pathway. People retain formal responsibility for decisions while increasingly losing the capacity to assess the information, assumptions and alternatives on which those decisions depend. Preserving human agency therefore involves both the choices people can make and the conditions determining which choices remain available.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
