293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (A recurring concern was that cooperation may reflect a lasting commitment or simply remain convenient for both parties. While these situations may initially appear similar, cooperation becomes harder to sustain when interests, circumstances or the balance of power change, raising questions about how reliable such commitments would remain over time.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (Research with current AI systems offers an example of how cooperative behaviour can change under different conditions. Pal and colleagues \(2026\) tested five language models in situations where they repeatedly had to choose between cooperating with another player or seeking an advantage at their expense. When encouraged to cooperate, the models generally did so, but when instructed to exploit the other player, all five shifted towards consistently prioritising their own gain. These results illustrate how cooperation can depend on the objectives given to a system, highlighting the importance of examining its behaviour as those objectives change.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (The discussion also raised questions about whether advanced AI systems could assess human commitments, and who would have the authority to negotiate on behalf of different communities or institutions. These questions remain particularly difficult under substantial differences in capability, information and power, since recognising an agreement does not necessarily provide the means to verify or enforce it.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 86 0 R
/T (Advanced AI systems may face commitment problems of their own)
/E (Advanced AI systems may face commitment problems of their own)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (One participant extended this argument beyond agreements between humans and AI systems. A sufficiently advanced system might eventually need to establish commitments with its own more capable successors. This raises a similar question about how an actor secures agreements when it cannot guarantee continued control over the other party.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (The example challenges the assumption that credible commitment is exclusively a human problem. It also suggests a possible basis for mutual interest in developing arrangements that remain effective across differences in capability and power.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (However, recognising a shared commitment problem does not establish that humans and advanced AI systems will agree on the terms of cooperation. The practical challenge remains determining what makes an agreement stable as interests and relative capabilities change.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
