752 0 obj
<<
/Title (This report is part of the Post-Control Initiative, developed through the Sentient Futures Project Incubator, which brought together researchers and practitioners for four expert roundtables held on 14, 15, 16, and 20 October 2026. This report focuses on two of these roundtables: Human Value and Claims in a Post-Control Scenario \(15 October\) and What Makes Cooperation Without Control Work \(20 October\). The discussions explored different perspectives on human influence and cooperation, including the difference between recognising human interests and being able to protect them.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 28 0 R
/Prev 751 0 R
/Next 418 0 R
>>
endobj
753 0 obj
<<
/Title (Recognising human rights and interests is not the same as ensuring they remain protected. Participants raised culture, relationships, autonomy and democratic life as interests worth preserving, but disagreed about what could protect them if humans lost economic leverage and could no longer enforce their claims. The discussion highlighted a central tension between recognising human value and ensuring that human interests remain protected when familiar sources of leverage weaken.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 418 0 R
/Next 1080 0 R
>>
endobj
754 0 obj
<<
/Title (Humans and advanced AI systems might cooperate even if humans could no longer reliably control AI, but sustaining that cooperation would require credible commitments. Shared goals give both sides reasons to work together without requiring identical values, while incentives and ways to detect broken agreements might help sustain trust. The unresolved challenge was how either side would verify that commitments were honoured over time, and what options humans would have if a more capable system broke them.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 418 0 R
/Prev 1080 0 R
>>
endobj
755 0 obj
<<
